messages:
nina: Quick heads-up 1 I opened a doc PR (gh#4780) to unify function/tool-calling examples and tidy up structured-output anchors. Main decisions needed: choose the canonical call frame shape, add minimal SDK snippets (py/js) for authors, and sweep broken anchors.
marc: Link? dropping in now.
nina: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/4780 1 I included failing anchors from the build: /docs/tooling#schema-listing -> should be /docs/tooling#schema-listing-ids, and /docs/structured-output#examples-json -> header id moved.
yuki: I reviewed the examples. There are three patterns in the docs: 1) flat call (top-level tool/args), 2) call_frame (single wrapper), 3) hybrid (wrapper with metadata). We should pick one as recommended. My vote is call_frame because it isolates future metadata without breaking callers.
emily: call_frame sounds good. Can we standardize on the key name `call_frame` (not envelope) to avoid copying other libs' terminology? Also add a tiny migration snippet that accepts both shapes.
marc: +1 to call_frame. We should also add a short note about arrays being nullable in some models: e.g., "results": [ ... ] vs null. That bit trips people up in parsing code.
nina: Agreed. Proposed minimal JSON Schema for the call_frame: 
```
{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "call_frame": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "tool": { "type": "string" },
        "params": { "type": ["object", "null"] }
      },
      "required": ["tool"]
    }
  },
  "required": ["call_frame"]
}
```
marc: LGTM. For params we purposely allow null so callers can handle empty args easily.
yuki: I'll add two compact snippets: one Python (sync) and one JS (async) showing generate + parse fallback. Python example I propose: 
```
# Python (pseudo)
resp = client.generate(input="Invoke tool", structured_output={"schema": CALL_FRAME_SCHEMA_ID})
text = resp.text
obj = parse_callframe_fallback(text)  # accepts call_frame and legacy flat
if 'call_frame' in obj:
  run_tool(obj['call_frame']['tool'], obj['call_frame'].get('params'))
else:
  # legacy
  run_tool(obj.get('tool'), obj.get('params'))
```
JS example: 
```js
// Node (pseudo)
const resp = await client.generate({input: 'Do thing', structured_output: {schema: CALL_FRAME_SCHEMA_ID}})
const parsed = parseCallframeFallback(resp.text)
if (parsed.call_frame) {
  await callTool(parsed.call_frame.tool, parsed.call_frame.params)
} else {
  await callTool(parsed.tool, parsed.params)
}
```
nina: Perfect. For the link fixes: marc, can you run a small HTML grep and replace and open a follow-up PR to update the broken anchors in the built site and the markdown sources? Add a tiny assertion to our link-checker that ensures header ids are kebab-case only.
marc: Yep. I can do a search for hrefs with old ids and map them to generated kebab-case ids. Also will add a step to normalize header text (remove backticks/parens) before id generation in our doc build script. That should fix flaky IDs.
yuki: One more request: add a short migration note in docs/clients/README: a 2-line recommendation and a code pointer to parseCallframeFallback.
emily: I'll add the README blurb and the SDK snippet files under docs/snippets/call_frame for py/js, and reference them in the main call-calling page.
docs-bot: Staging deploy: success. Link-check job queued.
marc: I'll wait for emily to push snippets then run link-check locally. If it passes I'll open docs/linkfix-anchors PR. Estimated 40m.
nina: Action list I tracked: emily -> snippets + README blurb; marc -> anchor sweep + link-checker tweak; yuki -> add schema and review examples; nina -> review final PR and merge once link-check is green.
emily: aiming to push in next 2 hours.
marc: tagging CI owner separately on the header-id normalization change.
docs-bot: Reminder: link-check will block merges if unresolved anchors exist.
